## Why This Is Asked

Gauges ability to manage per-tenant QoS under bursty traffic and to design practical safeguards that minimize blast radius.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant QoS and quotas
- Backpressure and prioritization
- Isolation and graceful degradation
- Telemetry, SLO-driven rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; this.burst = burst; this.tokens = burst; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + delta * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a service mesh with mutual TLS and header-based routing?
- How would you test failover and simulate tenant backoffs in a staging environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesss ability to architect latency-aware, resilient notify pipelines with per-region strategies, idempotence, and controlled degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-region traffic shaping, backpressure, local buffering
- Idempotent delivery and deduplication
- Fast-path caching and back-off strategies
- Canary deployments, runbooks, and rollback criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple idempotent delivery using Redis
const redis = require('redis');
const client = redis.createClient();

async function deliver(msgId, payload) {
  const key = `notif:${msgId}`;
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    client.setnx(key, 1, (err, res) => {
      if (err) return reject(err);
      if (res === 1) {
        client.expire(key, 3600, () => resolve(smsGateway.send(payload)));
      } else {
        resolve("duplicate");
      }
    });
  });
}
```

